{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Summary\n\nThe mcp blue-green deploy block documents itself as INDEPENDENT of the review-bot block above\nit, but it sits AFTER that block's `exit 0`. When the review-bot is busy, the deferral path\nterminates the whole script, so the mcp deploy never runs. The documented independence is false\nin the only case where it matters — a shared `src/rebar` change, which triggers BOTH triggers.\n\n## The coupling, in the code\n\n`infra/scripts/autodeploy.sh`:\n\n- `:140` `BOT_PATHS` and `:152` `MCP_PATHS` both begin with `src/rebar`, so any library change\n  matches BOTH.\n- `:506` `if changed \"$BOT_PATHS\" || changed \"$SECRETS_PATHS\"; then` — enters the bot block.\n- `:530-541` when `in_flight > 0` and the episode is under `DEPLOY_DEFER_MAX`, it emits\n  `AUTODEPLOY_DEFERRED` and **`exit 0`** — terminating the PROCESS, not just the bot block.\n- `:616-621` the mcp block's own header states:\n\n      # INDEPENDENT of the review-bot block above: a shared src/rebar / docker-compose.yml\n      # change triggers both, each on its own path. Unlike the review-bot's stop-and-drain,\n      # the mcp server is a never-idle shared endpoint ... and NEVER kills an in-flight\n      # certified op.\n\n  It is unreachable whenever the bot block deferred. The blue-green design exists precisely so\n  the mcp endpoint never waits on an in-flight review (`:120`), and that property is defeated by\n  control flow one screen above it.\n\n## Observed impact (2026-08-26)\n\nTwo commits merged at 06:47Z — `84fc5f9b68df` (library reads error on an absent store) and\n`30d7059f632d` (the mcp secrets-refresh fix). Forty minutes later neither was running: a live\nprobe of the deployed MCP server still returned the OLD behaviour (`list_tickets` -> `[]`\ninstead of erroring).\n\n`rebar/host` metrics, 60s period, show every tick deferring:\n\n    deploy_deferrals  07:12Z=3.0  07:18Z=3.0  07:24Z=2.0\n    deploy_errors     07:12Z=2.0  07:18Z=0    07:24Z=0\n\nThe observability probe runs every 5 min and autodeploy every 2 min, so 2-3 deferrals per probe\ninterval is ~100% of ticks.\n\nThe deferral marker is itself the proof the script got past the up-to-date check (`:470-476`)\nand matched a real delta — so this is not a stale `deployed-sha`, not a mirror-fetch failure,\nand not the SHA-keyed backoff (`:479-483`, which exits at `:481` BEFORE the deferral marker).\n\n**Why the bot is chronically busy:** all six open Gerrit changes sit at `LLM-Review = 0`. The\nreview bot drains a queue with a SINGLE worker (`review_bot/app.py:80`), so ordinary pipelining\nkeeps `in_flight > 0` essentially always. `autodeploy.sh:89-97` (bug 7b4a) already documents\nthat the bound \"is reasoned about as if it were a single-review timer\" and \"was outlasted by\nordinary PIPELINING\" — this ticket is that same saturation now blocking a path that was\nexplicitly designed not to wait.\n\n**The circularity worth naming:** `30d7059f632d` is itself the mcp deploy fix, and it cannot\ndeploy because of this. Change 2232 — which adds the `/health` store signal that would make a\nstoreless deploy diagnosable — is queued in the same saturated review queue.\n\n## Why the alarm did not catch it\n\n`rebar-autodeploy-errors` is Sum over 900s with 3-of-4 datapoints and `notBreaching` on missing\ndata, so an isolated failure cannot alarm by construction. It read OK throughout. A green alarm\nis not evidence of a healthy deploy here.\n\n## Proposed fix\n\nScope the deferral to the bot block instead of terminating the script: set a flag (e.g.\n`bot_deferred=1`) and `continue` past the remainder of the bot block, letting control reach the\nmcp block. The mcp path is a pointer-swap that never kills an in-flight op, so there is no\nreason for it to wait on the bot's drain.\n\nTake care that the deferral episode bookkeeping (`DEFER_FILE`, `:538`) and the\n`record_backoff_failure` semantics stay unchanged for the bot path, and that a bot deferral\nstill results in the same exit status the timer expects when the mcp block also does nothing.\n\n## Test gap that let this through\n\n`tests/scripts/test_autodeploy_mcp_bluegreen.py:144` stubs `git diff` to report ONLY\n`infra/compose/Dockerfile.mcp` as changed, so `changed \"$BOT_PATHS\"` is always false and the mcp\nblock always runs in isolation. The real case — a shared `src/rebar` change triggering both\nblocks while the bot is busy — is never exercised. The sibling suite\n`tests/scripts/test_autodeploy_review_drain.py` has coverage of the drain gate but none\nasserting the mcp target stays reachable across a deferral.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] A tick where the review-bot block DEFERS still reaches the mcp blue-green block, so a\n      shared `src/rebar` change deploys the mcp service without waiting on the bot's drain.\n- [ ] The bot path's deferral bookkeeping (episode file, bound, markers) and backoff semantics\n      are unchanged.\n- [ ] Regression coverage drives a tick with BOTH triggers matching and the bot busy, asserting\n      the mcp container is still deployed — the case the current stub makes unreachable.\n- [ ] The stub in `test_autodeploy_mcp_bluegreen.py` no longer forces bot-block-false, or a new\n      test supplies a git stub reporting a shared `src/rebar` change.\n\n## Verification\n\n    .venv/bin/python -m pytest tests/scripts/test_autodeploy_mcp_bluegreen.py tests/scripts/test_autodeploy_review_drain.py -q -p no:randomly\n    bash -n infra/scripts/autodeploy.sh && shellcheck -S error infra/scripts/autodeploy.sh\n\nThe new coverage has teeth when: with the `exit 0` restored in the deferral path, the new\nboth-triggers test FAILS (no mcp deploy), and with the scoped-`continue` fix it PASSES.\n## Scope growth across review rounds\n\nFour review rounds each found the same shape, each fix exposing the next: ONE piece of global\nstate standing in for TWO independently-deploying components.\n\n**Completion.** `deployed-sha` is written once, at the footer, so any early exit loses the\ncompletion of whichever component DID deploy and the next tick redeploys it — and a bot redeploy\nstop-and-drains, killing an in-flight review (bug 34cd). Each component now records its own\nmarker the moment it completes.\n\n**Failure.** `record_backoff_failure` wrote one `deploy-backoff` keyed on the global `$TARGET`\nand the top-level gate exited the whole script on it — so an mcp failure throttled the BOT and a\nbot failure suppressed mcp. mcp gets its own file; the existing one is scoped to the bot (every\ncall site is inside `deploy_review_bot`) and its gate moves into the bot block.\n\n**Incompleteness.** A component skipped by its own backoff window has not deployed, but nothing\nsaid so, so the footer stamped `$TARGET` into its marker for a deploy that never ran — after\nwhich the next tick computed no delta and dropped the change PERMANENTLY. `bot_incomplete` /\n`mcp_incomplete` mark it and the footer guard honours both. Each gate tests the DELTA before the\nwindow, keeping \"nothing to do\" distinct from \"skipped with work pending\".\n\n\n\n## Additional acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] An mcp failure does not write the SHARED backoff.\n      Proof: `test_mcp_failure_on_a_deferred_tick_does_not_backoff_the_bot`\n- [ ] An mcp failure does not cause the bot to redeploy on later ticks.\n      Proof: `test_a_failed_mcp_deploy_does_not_redeploy_the_bot_every_tick`\n- [ ] A bot skipped by its backoff window is not recorded as deployed.\n      Proof: `test_bot_backoff_tick_does_not_record_the_bot_as_deployed`\n- [ ] An mcp skipped by its backoff window is not recorded as deployed.\n      Proof: `test_mcp_backoff_tick_does_not_record_mcp_as_deployed`\n- [ ] An open backoff window with NO pending delta is not reported as a skip.\n      Proof: `test_an_open_bot_backoff_with_no_pending_delta_is_not_reported_as_a_skip`\n- [ ] The mcp backoff window skips mcp without stopping the tick.\n      Proof: `test_mcp_backoff_window_skips_the_mcp_deploy_without_stopping_the_tick`\n- [ ] mcp failures still emit `deploy_failed`, tagged `component=mcp`.\n      Proof: `test_mcp_failure_keeps_the_deploy_failed_metric_and_names_the_component`"}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787798070443063001,"uuid":"90e782ff-b298-4f45-bf46-74c86d9d126c"}